###### Article 511

2: The following terms mean:

1°) concealed access: the mechanism allowing access to data or an information system to be hidden without the authorization of the legitimate user;

2°) fraudulent access: any irregular mode of penetration of an automated data processing system;

3°) electronic communication: any emission, transmission or reception of signs, signals, writings, images or sounds, by electromagnetic means; any making available to the public or a category of the public by an electronic or magnetic communication process, of signs, signals, writings, images, sounds or messages of any nature;

4°) cybercrime: all criminal offenses committed using electronic communications networks and information systems or against said networks and systems;

5°) cryptography: application of mathematics allowing information to be written in such a way as to make it unintelligible to those who do not possess the capacity to decipher it;

6°) computer data: any representation of facts, information or concepts in a form suitable for computer processing, including a program capable of causing a computer system to perform a function;

7°) subscriber data: any information, in the form of computer data or in any other form, held by a service provider and relating to subscribers to its services, other than traffic or content data, and making it possible to establish:

a)	the type of communication service used, the technical arrangements taken in this regard and the period of service;

b)	the identity, electronic, postal or geographical address and telephone number of the subscriber, and any other access number, billing and payment data, available on the basis of a contract or service arrangement;

c)	any other information relating to the location of communication equipment, available on the basis of a contract or service arrangement;

8°) traffic data: all data relating to a communication passing through an information system, produced by the latter as an element of the communication chain, indicating the origin, destination, route, time, date, size and duration of the communication or the type of underlying service;

9°) fraudulent maintenance in a computer system: any irregular and continuous presence in an automated data processing system;

10°) racist and xenophobic material: any writing, any image or any other representation of ideas or theories which advocates or encourages hatred, discrimination or violence against a person or group of persons, on the basis of race, color, ancestry, affiliation or national or ethnic origin or religion, insofar as the latter serves as a pretext for one or other of these elements or which incites such acts;

11°) minor: any person under the age of eighteen within the meaning of the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child;

12°) child pornography: any data whatever its nature or form or medium representing:

a)	a minor engaging in sexually explicit behavior;

b)	a person who appears as a minor engaging in sexually explicit behavior;

c)	realistic images representing a minor engaging in sexually explicit behavior.

13°) computer program: sequence of instructions which specifies step by step the operations to be performed by a computer or computer component to obtain a result;

14°) direct marketing: any sending of a message intended to promote, directly or indirectly, goods, services or the image of a person selling goods or providing services;

15°) information system: organized set of resources (hardware, software, personnel, data and procedures) which allows information to be collected, grouped, classified, processed and disseminated;

16°) computer system: any device whether isolated or not, any set of interconnected or related devices, which ensures or one or more elements of which ensure automated data processing by executing a program;

17°) information and communication technologies: technologies used to collect, store, use and send information as well as those which involve the use of computers or any communication system including telecommunications;

18°) networks: systems for sharing information between several machines.

#### Chapter 2

Crimes and offenses related to information and communication technologies

##### Section 1: Attacks on the confidentiality of information systems

###### Paragraph 1: Fraudulent access to a computer system

###### Article 512

1: Whoever accesses or attempts to access fraudulently, all or part of an information system, is punished by imprisonment of one year and a fine of 5,000,000 francs.

Whoever obtains or attempts to obtain fraudulently, for himself or for another, any advantage by entering an information system is punished by the same penalties.

When it has resulted in either the deletion or modification of data contained in the system, or an alteration of the functioning of this system, the imprisonment is two years and the fine is 10,000,000 francs.

###### Paragraph 2: Fraudulent maintenance in a computer system

###### Article 512

2: Whoever maintains himself or attempts to maintain himself fraudulently in all or part of an information system, is punished by imprisonment of one year and a fine of 5,000,000 CFA francs.

When it has resulted in either the deletion or modification of data contained in the system, or an alteration of the functioning of this system, the imprisonment is two years and the fine is 10,000,000 francs.

##### Section 2

Attacks on the integrity and availability of information systems

###### Paragraph 1: Obstruction of the functioning of an information system

###### Article 512

3: Whoever intentionally obstructs or attempts to obstruct the functioning of an information system is punished by imprisonment of three years and a fine of 10,000,000 francs.

###### Paragraph 2: Fraudulent introduction of data into an information system

###### Article 512

4: Whoever introduces or attempts to introduce fraudulently data into an information system is punished by imprisonment of three years and a fine of 50,000,000 francs.

##### Section 3: Attacks on the integrity of data in an information system

###### Paragraph 1: Fraudulent interception of computerized data

###### Article 512

5: Whoever intercepts or attempts to intercept fraudulently computerized data during their non-public transmission to, from or within an information system, is punished by imprisonment of three years and a fine of 50,000,000 francs.

###### Paragraph 2: Fraudulent modification of computerized data

###### Article 512

6: Whoever intentionally modifies or attempts to modify, damages or attempts to damage, deletes or attempts to delete, erases or attempts to erase, alters or attempts to alter the data of an information system or their modes of processing or transmission is punished by imprisonment of three years and a fine of 50,000,000 francs.

