20: Is punished by imprisonment of one month and a fine of 500,000 francs without prejudice to damages, anyone who, in violation of the provisions of this chapter, installs and operates an aquaculture establishment.

### TITRE XI: OFFENCES RELATING TO PASTORAL ACTIVITIES

[Law No. 01-004/of 27 February 2001 on the Pastoral Charter of Mali]

#### Chapitre unique: Offences and penalties

###### Article 511

1: Is punished by a fine of 18,000 francs, without prejudice to damage caused to third parties, anyone who:

1°) occupies or obstructs a pastoral route or a stopover site or encroaches on their right of way;

2°) exploits water resources contrary to accepted rules or pollutes them;
3°) moves animals outside pastoral routes;

4°) contravenes the provisions relating to the opening periods of harvested fields for grazing animals.

###### Article 511

2: Is punished by imprisonment of six months and a fine of 100,000 francs, without prejudice to damage caused to third parties, anyone who:

1°) damages the property of others by allowing animals to roam freely;

2°) without an environmental impact study, implements a programme or project likely to result in the suppression or disappearance of pastoral resources, in whole or in part;

3°) contravenes a transhumance calendar.

### TITRE XII: OFFENCES IN MINING MATTERS

[Law No. 2023-040/of 29 August 2023 on the Mining Code in the Republic of Mali]

#### Chapitre unique: Offences and sanctions

Without prejudice to the provisions relating to the withdrawal of mining titles and authorizations and civil actions for compensation for damage caused, the following sanctions are applicable:

###### Article 512

1: Are punished by imprisonment of two years and a fine of 5,000,000 francs:

1°) those who make a false declaration to obtain a mining title;

2°) those who oppose in any manner whatsoever the occupation of a mining perimeter by the holder of the mining title;

3°) those who carry out the analysis of samples outside Mali without prior authorization from the administration in charge of mines;

4°) those who agree to have children work on their site.

###### Article 512

2: Are punished by imprisonment of three years and a fine of 5,000,000 francs:

1°) those who engage in research or exploitation work on mineral substances subject to the mining regime without holding an appropriate title;

2°) those who engage in illegal gold panning;

3°) those who provide aid and assistance to unauthorized prospectors and operators;

4°) those who do not declare, at the end of the mining title, the definitive cessation of work relating to site rehabilitation provided for by the regulations in force;

5°) those who hold mining titles and who do not comply within the prescribed time limit with the instructions of the administration in charge of Mines relating to measures for the protection of the safety and health of personnel, public safety and health, soil conservation, flora, fauna, communication routes, the solidity of public or private buildings, the flow or quality of water of any kind;

6°) those who hold mining titles and quarry exploitation authorizations who falsify their extraction, sale and/or shipment registers.

###### Article 512

3: Holders of large-scale or small-scale mining exploitation permits and holders of industrial quarry exploitation authorizations who do not keep their extraction, sale or shipment registers regularly, or who refuse to produce them to qualified officials and agents of the administration in charge of Mines, may, after a three-month formal notice by the minister in charge of Mines or the administration in charge of Mines that has remained unsuccessful, be deprived of their titles without prejudice to the payment of a fine of 5,000,000 francs.

###### Article 512

4: Is punished by imprisonment of three months and a fine of 2,000,000 francs:

1°) any person other than holders of mining titles and holders of authorization to open artisanal quarries or to exploit industrial quarries, executing a drilling, an underground work, an excavation work, whatever the purpose, with the exception of wells for domestic use, whose depth exceeds ten metres, who does not make the declaration either to the administration in charge of Mines, or to the Territorial Collectivity of the place where the work is carried out, which informs the administration in charge of Mines;

2°) any person who does not make the prior declaration to the administration in charge of Mines before any geophysical survey, any geochemical prospecting campaign or heavy mineral studies.

###### Article 512

5: Are punished by imprisonment of two years and a fine of 5,000,000 francs:

1°) those who, without a mining title and in an illicit manner, engage in the transport, processing and marketing of mineral substances extracted in Mali;

2°) those who exploit mineral substances subject to the quarry regime not covered by an authorization to open or exploit quarries;

3°) those who engage in the exploitation of mineral substances by dredging.

###### Article 512

6: Officials and sworn agents of the administration in charge of Mines may requisition public force agents.

Violence and assault exercised against officials and agents of the administration in charge of Mines in the exercise of their duties are punished by the penalties provided for by the provisions of this Code.

It is prohibited for any official or agent of the State in service in the public administration:

1°) to take directly or indirectly an interest in a research or exploitation enterprise of mineral substances over the entire extent of the territory;

2°) to exercise directly or through an intermediary service provisions with holders of research or exploitation mining titles;

3°) to disclose or give confidential information made available to him in the context of his work or even after cessation of service whatever the reason for a period elapsed of at least four years.

Any violation of these provisions results in the dismissal of the agent and the loss of civil servant status without prejudice to sanctions provided for by the competent courts.

